x86_64-windows  A closure type has no name the standard obliges anybody to match - [expr.prim.lambda] calls it a unique unnamed type. clang for this ABI spells the closure `<lambda_0>` and encodes its deduced return as `@`; cxx1 writes `$_0` and the deduced type, so the call operator is `??R$_0@?1??main@@9@QEBAHH@Z` against clang's `??R<lambda_0>@?0??main@@9@QEBA@H@Z`. Nothing links against either: a closure's call operator is reached through the object and the object never leaves the function that made it. The same divergence lambda.nonames records. Every other name in this case agrees, and x86_64-linux agrees on all eleven.
